<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Quadruple Alliance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1720">1720</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Quadruple Alliance (1718–1720) was fought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which under Philip V and his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni">Giulio Alberoni</a> had seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in defiance of the Utrecht settlement. The British fleet destroyed the Spanish navy off Cape Passaro in 1718 and French troops invaded northern Spain. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-hague-1720">Treaty of The Hague (1720)</a> Spain withdrew, and Austria exchanged Sardinia with Savoy for Sicily.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under the Peace of Utrecht of 1713, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to Savoy. Recovering them was a priority for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a>, a priority reinforced by his chief minister Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-alberoni">Giulio Alberoni</a> and by his second wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-farnese">Elisabeth Farnese</a>, both natives of Parma. </p><p>Utrecht barred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from ever uniting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and Philip gave up any claim to the French throne. After deaths in the French royal family in 1711 and 1712 made him heir presumptive to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, he cast doubt on that renunciation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France agreed an alliance in 1716 and formed the Triple Alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in January 1717 to enforce the terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Spanish forces landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in August 1717 and held it by November, meeting little opposition. In July 1718 the Spanish landed 30,000 men on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. By then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and joined the Quadruple Alliance. On 11 August a British squadron under Sir George Byng destroyed the Spanish fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cape-passaro">Battle of Cape Passaro</a>. An Austrian force was defeated at the First Battle of Milazzo on 15 October. After the discovery of the Cellamare conspiracy, Alberoni's plot to replace the regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with Philip V, France declared war, and by 17 December 1718 France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Austria were all at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> joined in August 1719.</p><p>In April 1719 a French army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fitzjames-1st-duke-of-berwick">Duke of Berwick</a> invaded the Basque districts and besieged San Sebastián. An expedition under the Duke of Ormonde to put James Stuart on the British throne was scattered by a storm in March 1719, and a small force of Spanish marines was defeated with its Highland allies at Glen Shiel in June. A British raid took Vigo in October. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> the Austrians under Count de Mercy lost at Francavilla on 20 June 1719 but won the second Battle of Milazzo, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in October and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Philip dismissed Alberoni in December 1719 and made peace by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-hague-1720">Treaty of The Hague</a> on 17 February 1720. He relinquished all territory captured in the war, but his third surviving son's right to the Duchy of Parma and Piacenza was recognised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> and its conquests in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in exchange for commercial benefits, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. The war was unusual in the 18th century in having <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France on the same side.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Quadruple Alliance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
